#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:0
msgid ""
"Reference documentation for the personal-files interface, which grants snaps "
"privileged access to specified files and directories, including hidden ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:8
msgid "personal-files interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:10
msgid ""
"The `personal-files` interface provides access to specified files in the "
"user's home directory, and consequently, gives privileged access to the "
"user's data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:12
msgid ""
"This interface is typically used to provide read-only access to top-level "
"hidden data directories (directories starting with a dot) within a user's "
"real home directory in order to support importing data from existing "
"applications where the snap is the clear owner of the target directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:14
msgid ""
"By default, snaps have access to everything under `~/snap/<snap name>/"
"<revision>` and it's this path stored in the `$SNAP_USER_DATA` {ref}"
"`environment variable <reference-development-environment-variables>`. For "
"non-daemon commands within the snap, this location is also where the `$HOME` "
"environment variable points to, and where they can read and write to hidden "
"files freely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:16
msgid "Requires snapd version _2.37+_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:19
msgid "Developer details"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:21
msgid ""
"**{ref}`Auto-connect <explanation-interfaces-interface-auto-connection>`**: "
"no</br> **{ref}`Super-privileged <reference-operations-interfaces-super-"
"privileged-interfaces>`**: yes</br> **Transitional**: no</br> **Attributes**:"
"</br>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:21
msgid "</br>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:25
msgid ""
"`read` (plug): list of files and/or directories for read-only access (eg, "
"'`read: [ $HOME/.file-read, $HOME/.dir-read ]`'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:26
msgid ""
"`write` (plug): list of files and/or directories for read/write access (eg, "
"'`write: [ $HOME/.file-write, $HOME/.dir-write ]`'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:28
msgid ""
"Specifying a directory in `read` and `write` allows access to the directory "
"and all files under it. If there are missing directories between `$HOME` and "
"the target file or directory, these are created automatically when the "
"associated application is first run by a non-root user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:30
msgid "Approval process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:32
msgid ""
"For distribution via the [Snap store](https://snapcraft.io/store), snaps "
"that use the personal-files interface need an approved {ref}`snap "
"declaration <interfaces-process-for-aliases-auto-connections-and-tracks>`. "
"For acceptance, the publisher needs to make a descriptive interface "
"reference, as used by `snap connections|interfaces|connect|disconnect` "
"commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:34
msgid "Code examples"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:36
msgid ""
"If a *foo* application is being packaged as a snap and its publisher wants "
"the snap to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:37
msgid ""
"Import an existing configuration from `~/.config/foo` into "
"`$SNAP_USER_DATA/.config/foo` (ie, `$HOME/.config/foo` within the snap's "
"runtime environment or `~/snap/foo/<revision>/.config/foo`) on the host)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:38
msgid ""
"Save user-specific data in `$HOME/.local/share/foo`, where `$HOME/.local` "
"and `$HOME/.local/share/` will be automatically created for the calling user "
"if it does not exist"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:40
msgid "The *snapcraft.yaml* could include the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:42
msgid ""
"name: foo\n"
"...\n"
"plugs:\n"
"  dot-config-foo:\n"
"    interface: personal-files\n"
"    read:\n"
"    - $HOME/.config/foo\n"
"  dot-local-share-foo:\n"
"    write:\n"
"    - $HOME/.local/share/foo\n"
"\n"
"apps:\n"
"  foo:\n"
"    plugs:\n"
"    - dot-config-foo\n"
"    slots:\n"
"    - dot-local-share-foo\n"
"    ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:63
msgid ""
"When declaring an instance of the `personal-files` plug or slot, it should "
"be named with a descriptive name that indicates to a user what access it "
"grants. In the above example, `dot-config-foo` is used to reflect the access "
"to `~/.config/foo`, and `dot-local-share-foo` is used to reflect the access "
"to `~/.local/share/foo`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:65
msgid ""
"You would then be able to use the following to enable access to personal "
"files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/personal-files-interface.md:67
msgid "snap connect foo:dot-config-foo\n"
msgstr ""
